# Cab Lab usage log (read before guessing)

When the user reports something that went wrong in The Cab Lab (box placed at the wrong place, wrong size, inference not engaging, handle drag misbehaving, crash, file not opening…), **read the log first**; do not ask them to re-describe coordinates or steps.

## Files (all under `the-cab-lab/logs/`, git-ignored)

1. `latest.json` — last action and last error of the current session. Start here.
2. `usage.jsonl` — append-only event stream, one JSON object per line (`seq`, `t`, `kind`, …). Read the last ~50 lines; the event the user is talking about is normally the last `place.finish` / `handle.end` / `error`.
3. `crash-<time>.json` — full `job.json` + cursor trace written on `window.error` / unhandled rejection.

`Ctrl+Shift+L` in the app opens this folder.

## Event kinds

- `session.start` — version, viewport.
- `place.arm` / `place.anchor` / `place.face` / `place.corner` / `place.typein` / `place.trace` / `place.finish` / `place.repeat` / `place.cancel` / `place.blocked` / `place.unfit` — one box placement in three steps (anchor on a face → opposite corner of a flat rectangle on that face → extrude along the face normal, away from the solid only). Side walls are only pickable from the room inside. `place.anchor` / `place.face` carry `plane` `{axis, value, dir, label}` (`place.face` = a corner anchor switched to the face being drawn over). `place.finish` carries `how` (click / enter / `*.preset` / click.locked / repeat), `anchor`, `corner`, `locked` (typed dims), `plane`, `extrude` (`len`, snap `label`), `clamped` (dims stopped by the space, with the wall name) and the final `box` (x0/y0/z0/W/D/H). `place.trace` is the last 60 cursor samples: `cx/cy` client px, `x/y/z` resolved world mm, `kind` = `feature` | `inference` | `align` | `plane` | `extrude` | `extrude.snap` (with `len`, `label`), `dir`, `shift`.
- **Kitchen / ensuite placement** uses the ordinary box (anchor on a face → rectangle → extrude). Enter on the rectangle (`rb.step` `face`) does not create: it locks the face and starts the depth pull (`place.corner`, then `extrude`). Enter or a click during the pull places the cabinet. A typed size locks that dimension (`place.typein` `locked` is the number), including when it matches where the cursor already is. The mouse no longer changes it. Clearing the field and Tab commits the empty value and unlocks it (`locked` null). A face change keeps a typed lock.
- `move.start` / `move.kind` / `move.target` / `move.finish` / `move.copy` / `move.cancel` — Move (M). One command, three ways to choose, picked on the card (`kind`): `free` (arrows and rings), `face` (two faces, same direction, the first moves onto the second's plane), `point` (a corner onto another point, no rotation). `target` is `module` or `panel`, same rules as before. `move.kind` is switching that page; it throws away the unconfirmed slide. Face picks log `align.pick` / `align.finish` / `align.blocked`; point picks log `pointalign.pick` / `pointalign.finish` / `pointalign.blocked`. Those are not separate commands and do not make their own undo step — Enter is `move.finish`, Esc is `move.cancel`, one step for the whole Move. Ctrl+Enter copies a module. A module with `attach` (bed box, bed side table) keeps the pose the body gives it — use Panel. A board nudge is `cabinet.overrides.boards[roleId]`. `move.finish` carries `from` / `to` pose, `clamped` when the room stopped a module.
- `orient.start` / `orient.pending` / `orient.blocked` / `orient.end` / `orient.cancel` — the Face command (`face` `{axis, dir, label}` clicked, `from` / `to` `{pose, envelope}` — the box never moves, W/D swap; `blocked` = side against a wall / neighbour, `small` = W/D under the module minimum; `how` = click / enter, `changed`).
- `place.finish` also carries `side` `{axis, dir}` (door side is across the vehicle: the back faces the outside (left wall on the left half, right wall on the right half; a side wall the box is flush with wins), the door face faces the inside (±X toward the centre). At a rear corner — the box flush with the back wall and a side wall — the back sits on the rear and the doors face forward (`side` `{axis:"y", dir:-1}`). Kitchen, ensuite, tall, fridge, small cabinet and overhead all use this), `size` `{W, D, H}` in module terms and the resulting `pose`.
- Overhead (`moduleId: overheadCabinet`, ceiling-hung): `place.anchor` carries `walls` (the ceiling ∩ wall lines the anchor sits on) and `term` (`{x, y, z}` → which module dimension W/D/H runs along each world axis once the back wall is known); `place.face` carries `term` too; `place.finish` carries `wall` (the back wall's label; W runs along it, doors face away from it). `place.blocked` with `reason: "not on a ceiling edge"` = the click was not on a feature point where the flat ceiling meets a space wall, a cabinet's outer box, or a partition (the board stops short of the roof; the feature point is the extension, and a click on the board's own top — one ceiling clearance below — is that same roof point, not a second point). There is no face on that short top, so pulling a box down from the ceiling is not caught there. At a corner the back wall is decided at the end: the wall the longer horizontal edge follows. Since 2026-10-08 the overhead is drawn like the kitchen rectangle (`quietPlace()` in `interact.js`): no text follows the cursor while the box is drawn (the rectangle still stops on walls, partitions and cabinets), Enter on the rectangle only starts the height pull (mode `extrude`, nothing created), and the next click or Enter places it — with the preset height when nothing was pulled or typed. Typed sizes lock as in the kitchen. The U overhead keeps the old behaviour (Enter on the rectangle creates with the preset height).
- `orient.blocked` with `reason: "module has a fixed door side"` / `key.rotate` with `blocked` — Face and `R` are refused on an overhead. Move still translates and rotates it on X Y Z.
- **U overhead** (`moduleId: uShapeOverheadCabinet`, ceiling-hung): three straight overheads. `totalWidth` runs along the back wall, `leftArmLength` / `rightArmLength` are the overall depths of the arms, `cabinetDepth` is the carcass depth of each run. The back run owns both corners (`SIDE_CLEARANCE_MM` 50 plus the door thickness). A `rangehood_flap` on BACK is rewritten to `up_flap` with a warning. A hood on a side arm stays outside that corner. `uohc.set` (`key`, `from`, `to`) is a panel edit. The drawn boards are the three runs in one frame (y = 0 at the open tips). A run that is too short is a `generator.errors` line, and two runs that intersect are `LEFT.BP overlaps BACK…`.
- `ohc.zone.select` / `ohc.zone.drag` (`boundary`, `changed`, `widths` — one boundary drag on the strip, one undo step) / `ohc.zone.type` (`zone`, `type`) / `ohc.zone.width` (`zone`, `width` the stored span, `mode` `clear`|`center`, `shown` the clearance or centre distance typed, `where` `"front view"` (the number under a zone) | `"zone card"`; the stored span moves by the difference from the number on screen and the zone to the right — the left one for the last zone — takes the rest; the card cuts an out-of-range number back to the 150 mm limit, the number under the front view refuses it (`ohc.zone.blocked`); the strip, the card and the front view all show the dropdown's reading) / `ohc.zone.add` (`at`, `from` the selected zone, `widths`, `splitAfter` — since 2026-10-08 it inserts to the right of the one selected zone and takes up to half of it, at most 400; zones further right keep their widths; a split on or right of that line moves one index along so it stays on the same x; refused — greyed button with the reason as its tooltip — with no single selection, inside a range hood group, when the zone cannot give 150 and keep 150, or when the result would add a generator error such as a hood group narrower than 635) / `ohc.zone.remove` (`removed`, `splitAfter` — each removed zone's width goes to the nearest kept zone on its left, the first zone's to its right; the split stays only when the two zones across it survive) / `ohc.zone.average` (`zones`) / `ohc.zone.blocked` / `ohc.rangehood` (`key` `rangehoodClearHeight` \| `rangehoodAlignment` \| `rangehoodEdgeOffsetX`, `from`, `to`) / `ohc.split` (`on`, `after` the zone index to the left of the joint, `x`, `from` when removed) / `ohc.split.move` (`from`, `to`, `x`, `changed`, `where: "zone strip"`) — Split on a line between zones, never through a rangehood group and never on an outer end. Two CPT dividers butt there; flaps each keep half the clearance. A run over 2400 mm warns until both sides fit a sheet. `ohc.option` (`key` `style` \| `ledGroove` \| `topClearanceHeight` \| `hingeHoleDiameter` \| `hingeHoleDepth` \| `hingeHoleFromTop` \| `hingeHoleFromSide`, `from`, `to`) — the overhead editor page (right panel, 714 wide like the kitchen). Its front view is the kitchen-style elevation (`generateOHCFrontView` in `generators/overheadCabinet/svgPreview.ts`): click a zone to select it, click the number under a zone to type it; widths sit above the bottom panel, heights up each zone's left edge, Clearance or Centre to centre one at a time. `widths` always sum to `cabinetWidth`; none is under 150. A zone type `rangehood_flap` is the NCE range hood: adjacent hood zones are one insert (`RGHD_TOP` / `RGHD_FRONT` / `RGHD_BACK`, 555 × 285 through the bottom). A refused hood is a `generator.errors` line (carcass depth under 365, clear width under 635, two separate groups, or the insert hits the top rails). A "wrong zone" report is normally the last of these plus the `handle.end` of a vertical divider bar (`zones` there lists widths for an overhead).
- `tall.zone.select` (`zone` = the `zoneItem.id` such as `zone-2`, null = deselect) / `tall.zone.drag` (`boundary` `"zone"` with `index` (the gap after zone `index`) or `"dividerCenterX"` with `zone` (x drag of a vertical divider centreline), `from`, `to` (zone `height` list or the centre x), `changed`, `where: "front view"` — one drag, one undo step) / `tall.zone.<kind>` (`zone`, kind `type`|`add`|`remove`|`height`|`appliance`|`divider`|`shelf`|`shelfHeight` — toolbar + zone card edits; `add` puts an open zone on top and takes up to 300 from the tallest zone (`from`), `remove` gives the height to the zone below (`heir`), so the stack total never changes) / `tall.zone.blocked` (`reason` — no zone could give 60). The zone grip sits on the **centre line of the boundary panel** between two zones (`to` is where that centre line went) — the tall-cabinet editor page (`renderTall`, front elevation). The stack is read from `result.stack` (`bottom_system` / `zoneItem` / `boundary_panel` / `top_system`); a zone's z range is the row's `z0`/`z1`. A "wrong zone height" report is the last `tall.zone.drag` plus the zone's `height` in `cabinet.params`. `tall.preset` (`preset` id, `from` / `to` envelope) = the Tall panel's Preset dropdown applied a named cabinet from `generators/generalTall/presets.json` (`ui: true`; since 2026-10-01 fridge presets are only on the Fridge panel and log `tallFridge.preset`; today `rogue-dometic`, the 21 Bunk Dometic fridge 593 × 640 × 1965): every param is replaced except the door colours and grain. The carcass back stays on the wall (local y+); the side away from the side panel stays; the front moves (`fromPose` → `toPose`). Storage still grows from `corner` (`{x,y,z}` ±1 in the cabinet frame): `cabinet.placeCorner`, the box corner the user clicked first when drawing it (also on `cabinet.add` as `placeCorner`), else (`stored: false`) the back-left floor corner; `rotZ` never changes (`fromPose` → `toPose`). A tall's box D is carcass depth (`cabinetDep