- `nose.arm` / `nose.drag` / `nose.typein` / `nose.finish` / `nose.cancel` — Bedroom (nose slab) placement: `arm` carries `depth` (preset 700 or the existing slab's) and `editId` (non-null = the module was picked again and the existing slab is being re-sized, nothing is duplicated); `drag` = the click that started the room-side face following the cursor; `finish` carries `how` (click / enter), `depth`, `locked`, `snap` (label), `clamped` (`back wall` / `minimum N` / a cabinet id), `edited`, `changed`, `pose` (always `rotZ 180`, `y = depth`), `envelope` and the bound `roofProfile` (local Y from the room face). Width / height of the slab are never typed: they come from the space, so a "wrong height" is a space (roof profile) question, not a placement one.
- **Bedroom body layout** (`generators/bedroom`, right panel `renderBedroom`): the body is five regions — `boot` (wall to wall, floor → `bootHeight`; made of three boards `BOOT_DECK` / `BOOT_BACK` / `BOOT_FRONT`), `wardrobeL` / `wardrobeR` (side wall → `wardrobeWidth`, boot deck → roof; Style 1 also `WARD_L/R_FIXED` + `WARD_L/R_DOOR`; nook `WARD_L/R_KICK` + `WARD_L/R_FLOOR` + `WARD_L/R_NOOK` + `WARD_L/R_DOOR`), `opening` (a void between them, boot deck → `ohcBottom`) and `ohc` (above the opening, to the roof). Symmetric by rule: one `wardrobeWidth` moves both faces. The opening must take the **bed frame** (`bedFrame`, `queen` = `BED_FRAME_QUEEN_WIDTH_MM` 1508): the wardrobes stop at `(W − 1508) / 2`. Wardrobe fronts: `style` `style1` (default) | `nook` (door over an open nook). Style 1 stores/drags `fixedPanelTop` (the split); nook does not drag a wardrobe bottom: it is `bootHeight` + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197 + `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_HEIGHT_MM` 204 (Style 3: 799). The door starts there (no + 4), the shelf (carcass 15, `WARDROBE_NOOK_SHELF_NOSE_GAP_MM` 22 short of the nose) sits on it, the wall strip stands on the shelf top and has no notch, the base is kick + floor (`WARDROBE_BASE_THICKNESS_MM` 18 each, floor top = boot + 197) and 595 → 799 on the room face is open — a "hole under the door" report in nook is the design, not a missing board. Each colour panel (`WARD_L/R_PANEL`) is cut through on the room edge between the floor top and that bottom: a fixed U, floor reach `nook.cut.floorY` 611.3, crest at `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_UPPER_CENTER_Y_MM` 502.775, arcs `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_UPPER_RADIUS_MM` 100 and `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_LOWER_RADIUS_MM` 703.374. Raising the boot moves the whole U; its height and depth stay put. A "cut missing" report on a nook colour panel is this outline, and there is no `nookShelfBottom` boundary to drag. `ledGroove` (default true; panel checkbox "LED channels", `bedroom.layout.set` with `key: "ledGroove"`, `how: "toggle"`) adds `tgroove` features `for: "led"` on face A of every T3 (`*_LED_MAIN` 14.5 × 6.5 full width, back wall `LED_T3_T1_GAP_MM` 0.5 in front of T1's front → Style 3 v 20 → 34.5; `*_LED_BRANCH_1/2` 20 wide (`LED_T3_BRANCH_WIDTH_MM`), centre `LED_T3_BRANCH_END_INSET_MM` 30 from each end of that board, from the main channel to the rear edge) and a `groove` `WARD_L/R_NOOK_LED` on face B of each nook shelf (centred, from `LED_NOOK_SHELF_FROM_ROOM_FACE_MM` 54 to the rear). A "channel in the wrong place" report is one of those rules; a "channel missing" report is the checkbox or a `generator.errors`/`warnings` line (`LED channel skipped: …`). The shelf depth is `depth − WARDROBE_NOOK_SHELF_NOSE_GAP_MM` 22, or less when the roof meets the shelf top first (`nook shelf stops at N` warning) — a "shelf shorter than the nose" report on a low nose is that, and a `generator.errors` line `the roof comes down to N at the nook shelf's back` was the old refusal (seq 24, 2026-09-23: roof 786.6 at y 748, shelf top 814, body depth 770). 3D draws every groove as a pocket, also on outlined boards (`boardGeom.js pocketGeometry`; touching T rectangles are merged into one hole); wardrobe floor = `bootHeight` + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197; door = split + `WARDROBE_DOOR_CLEARANCE_MM` 4 → T3 top; door hangs at y −`doorPanelThickness`…0 (envelope `frontPanelThickness` stays 0; front view is still the y = 0 elevation). `bedroom.layout.drag` (`key`, `side` ±1 for the mirrored pair, `from`, `to`, `changed`, `where: "front view"` — one boundary drag on the panel's front elevation, one undo step) / `bedroom.layout.set` (`key` incl. `bedFrame` / `style` / `fixedPanelTop` / `nookShelfBottom` / `ledGroove` / `ohcZones`, `from`, `to`, `how: "type" | "select"`, `clamped` = the `{min, max}` the other values left when the typed number was cut back, `changed`). `ohcZones` is 2 or 3 up-flap bays whose widths sum to the opening; a drag with `key: "ohcZone"` moves one centreline (`to` is the resulting zone list). The same layout numbers are the orange bars in 3D (standoff ~20 so they sit in front of the 16 mm doors): `handle.start` / `handle.end` with `handle: "divider"` carry `key` and the resulting `value` (index 0 boot deck, 1 overhead bottom, 2 / 3 wardrobe faces, 4 / 5 Style 1 fixed-panel split (nook has no wardrobe-bottom bar), then one bar per overhead bay boundary). Middle overhead boards are `OHC_BP` / `OHC_T3` / `OHC_D*` / `OHC_FP*`. `ohcBottom` is the door underside; the bottom panel is 30 above it and is cut 18 mm past the roof (`OHC_BP_OVERSIZE_MM`) — a "panel too deep" report is that allowance, not the uprights. No T4. A "wrong hinge" on an up flap is `OHC_HINGE_FROM_SIDE_2_MM` 150 (two bays, from the side-panel outer face and the centre divider) or `OHC_HINGE_FROM_SIDE_3_MM` 100 (three bays, from the door edge), 22.5 from the top. `select` carries `region` (`boot` | `wardrobeL` | `wardrobeR` | `opening` | `ohc`) when a region was picked in the front view or by a second 3D click — the body is still the selected object; `board` / `face` when a board was picked (`BOOT_*`, `WARD_L/R_STRIP` carcass wall strips (175 deep, boot deck, same top as the colour panel; Style 1 through notch y 75 → 175 for the shelf), `WARD_L/R_SHELF` Style 1 carcass shelves (10 above the wardrobe floor, tongue into the colour panel), `WARD_L/R_PANEL` colour panels, `WARD_L/R_T3`, `T2`, `T1`, `WARD_L/R_FIXED`, `WARD_L/R_DOOR`, nook `WARD_L/R_KICK` / `WARD_L/R_FLOOR` / `WARD_L/R_NOOK`). The wardrobe top hangs on one Y (`WARDROBE_T2_BACK_MM` 66) and the roof there: `layout.top` in the result gives `seat`, `t3Top`, `roofAtT2`, `t2Height`; `layout.front` gives Style 1 `floorTop` / `fixedPanelTop` / `doorBottom` / `doorTop` / `clearance`. A "T3 / colour panel step at the wrong height" report is the roof profile at y 66 (the space) or `WARDROBE_T2_HEIGHT_MM`, not a layout param. A "wrong door height / split" is `fixedPanelTop` (Style 1) / `nookShelfBottom` (nook) or T3 top; a "wrong hinge cup" is `WARDROBE_HINGE_*` (Ø35 ×