- **East-west bedroom boards** (`generators/bedroomEast/boards.ts`, built as `Bedroom 1.smt`; `bedroom1.model.json` holds the model's 39 board boxes and the test holds every board within 0.5 mm): `BOOT_DECK` / `BOOT_BACK` / `BOOT_FRONT`; wardrobe `WARD_PANEL` (door stock, colour face A toward the bed, shelf groove on B), `WARD_STRIP` (150 deep, shelf notch), `WARD_SHELF` (floor + 10, tongue 240 into the panel), `WARD_FIXED_BACK`, `WARD_FIXED`, `WARD_DOOR` (3 cups, lock slot 16 × 55 with r 8 ends, through); top rails `T1` (roof + 20) / `T2` (back at body face + 65) / `T3` (one board wall to wall, 205 deep, notched 0.5 each side of every board rising through it, 1 at a wall; LED main 14.5 × 6.5 at body face + 14 and branches 30 from each end, `ledGroove`); overhead `OHC_BP` (door underside + 30, grooves 7.5 deep), `OHC_D0`…`OHC_Dn` (tongue 150), `OHC_FILLER` (115 at the right wall) + `OHC_FILLER_BACK`, `OHC_FP*` (edge gap 2, 3.5 between doors, cups 100 from each edge); bedside cabinet `BS_*` (200 deep in front of the wardrobe, 615 high, drawer over a door, lock slots 54.5 × 16.2 with r 8.1 ends — a "square lock slot" report is a missing `radius` on the cutout); bed box `BB_*` (18 stock, from wardrobe + 65 to the wall, half-laps, `BB_CHAMFER` + `BB_CHAMFER_IN` = the 38.9° corner, drawn in plan). A "board not like the model" report: compare with `bedroom1.model.json`.
- **East-west bedroom** (`moduleId: bedroomEast`, `generators/bedroomEast`, rail Bedroom → East-west): layout regions with boards (above). `nose.arm` / `nose.finish` with `depth` always `MATTRESS_DEPTH_MM` 1570 (fixed, one click creates it). Local Y runs from the room face (0) to the nose (1570): `boot` (full width, y 814 → 1570, top `BOOT_HEIGHT_MM` 418) and `wardrobe` (x 0 → `wardrobeWidth`, y 814 → 1570, boot top → roof) stand against the nose (`BODY_DEPTH_MM` 756); `mattress` (x `wardrobeWidth` → W, y 0 → 814, top 418) runs on into the room. Width, height and `roofProfile` come from the space (`withSpace`, same as north-south). `wardrobeWidth` defaults to W − `MATTRESS_QUEEN_LENGTH_MM` 1880 − `BED_SIDE_GAP_MM` 65 (330 on 2275) and can only be made narrower (min 150); the bed box starts at wardrobe + 65, so a narrower wardrobe lengthens the mattress. A van under 2095 wide builds with a warning `the mattress is only N mm long`. Regions: `boot`, `wardrobe`, `bedside` (x 0 → wardrobe, y 614 → 814), `bedbox` (x wardrobe + 65 → W, y 0 → 814), `opening`, `ohc`; each lists its boards. Overhead: region `ohc` from the wardrobe face to the right wall, y 814 → where the roof meets its underside, `ohcBottom` (door underside, default `OHC_BOTTOM_DEFAULT_MM` 1418, limits boot + `OPENING_HEIGHT_MIN_MM` 500 → roof at y 814 − `OHC_HEIGHT_MIN_MM` 150) → roof; `ohcZones` 2 or 3 up-flap bays (default 3 equal, each ≥ 150), widths sum to W − wardrobe and re-scale when the wardrobe moves; `result.layout.ohc.zones` gives each bay's x0 / x1. `opening` is the void under it (boot top → `ohcBottom`, cut by the roof). No boards yet. `bedroomEast.layout.set` (`key` wardrobeWidth | ohcBottom | ohcZones, `from`, `to`, `how`) / `handle.end` with `handle: "divider"`: index 0 = wardrobe face, 1 = overhead underside, 2+ = bay boundaries (`key: "ohcZone"`). A "wardrobe through the roof" report is a missing `roofProfile` (the nose.finish `roofProfile` should follow the space's nose).
- `bedbox.arm` / `bedbox.blocked` / `bedbox.typein` / `bedbox.finish` / `bedbox.cancel` — Bed Box placement (needs the Bedroom body: `blocked` = no body). The box stands in the body's opening and runs into the room: `W` = the body's bed frame width (queen 1508), `H` = the body's `bootHeight` — both read from the body (`arm` carries `from`), never typed or dragged; only the length `D` is chosen (default `LENGTH_DEFAULT_MM` 979 in `generators/bedBox/rules.json`). `arm` carries `editId` (non-null = re-sizing the existing one), `bodyId`, `bodyDepth` and the starting `W/D/H`; `finish` carries `how`, `W`, `D`, `H`, `locked` (`{D}`), `snap`, `clamped` (`back wall` / `minimum N` / a cabinet id in the bed's lane), `edited`, `changed`, `pose` (`rotZ 180`, `x = centre + W/2`, `y = bodyDepth + D`). The bed box is 12 boards (`SIDE_L/R`, `END`, `DIVIDER`, `RAIL_L/R_LOW/HIGH`, `RAIL_END/BODY_LOW/HIGH`, all 18 stock; notches and the 1 mm oversize / gaps are `generators/bedBox/rules.json`). A "wrong bed width / height" report is a body question (`bedroom.layout.*`: bed frame / boot height), a "wrong notch / rail" report is a bedBox rule; an "off-centre bed" is a space-width or body question. `attach()` re-reads W / H whenever the body changes.
- `plane.arm` / `plane.blocked` / `plane.face` / `plane.typein` / `plane.finish` / `plane.cancel` / `plane.add` / `plane.remove` — construction plane: click a wall or cabinet face, offset a parallel copy into the room (`dir` of the source face). `face` carries `axis/value/dir/label/source/room`; `finish` / `add` carry `id`, `axis`, `value` (world), `offset`, `from` `{source, label}`. The plane is world-fixed (does not follow the source). Its outline vertices (plane ∩ space) are snap points. `blocked` = no space, or the face has no room outward.
- **Board command = a sketch on one face** (B, command bar; `renderer/boardSketch.js`). Steps `pick` → `sketch` → `stock` (`getMode()` `board.<step>`). `board.arm` (`aids`) / `board.face` (`{axis, value, dir, label, source}` — the click only picks the face; nothing is drawn yet) / `board.tool` (`tool` — chosen on the **sketch bar** at the top of the view, icons with the name on hover: Create = Line (L), Rectangle (R), Circle (C, or C closes a line being drawn), Arc (A: start, end, a point on it) | a divider | Modify = Fillet (F), Chamfer (K), Offset (O), Mirror (M), Trim (T), Extend (E); a Construction icon on the right of that bar — hold Shift while drawing a line, rectangle, circle or arc, or click the icon, and that shape is a green dashed guide (`construction` count on `board.shape` / `board.line` / the other sketch commits; `board.guide` `{on}` when the icon is clicked). Guides are snap targets and are left out of the boards; then Finish sketch / Cancel; `select` = the default, click a line or shape) / `board.select` (`index` of the item, or null when the click missed; `closed`, `construction`) / `board.delete` (Delete on the selection: `index`, `closed`, `construction`) / `board.move` (drag the selection: `index`, `du`, `dv`) ; null in older logs meant no tool, the button now logs `select`) / `board.point` (`tool`, `index`, `u`/`v` on the face, `snap` close|endpoint|midpoint|center|geocenter|quadrant|intersection|extension|perpendicular|tangent|nearest|parallel|feature|ortho|polar|align|grid|typed kind, `how` click|typein|enter|right-click|cursor) / every change to the sketch is one entry with `tool`, `items`, `closed`: `board.shape` (rectangle, circle, arc or a closed line), `board.line` (a line ended open with Enter / right-click), `board.fillet` / `board.chamfer` (`vertex`), `board.offset` (`from`, `d`), `board.mirror` (`from`), `board.trim`, `board.extend`; `board.pick` (`tool`, `index` — the item Offset / Mirror works on); `board.size` (`tool` and its sizes: fillet `r`, chamfer `d1` `d2`, offset `d`, shown in the fields beside the cursor while the tool is on; typing d1 sets d2 too). The sketch holds **items** (`renderer/sketchCurves.js`): open lines and closed curves, edges straight or arcs (bulge = tan(sweep/4)); after each change open lines whose ends meet (0.5 mm) join, and one whose two ends meet closes. Rectangles and circles may overlap while sketching; Trim cuts the piece of the clicked edge between the nearest crossings (or up to its corner), so two overlapping outlines trim into one. Extend runs an open straight end on to the first edge it meets. Fillet / Chamfer act on a corner between two straight edges and say the largest size that fits. Offset: click the item, then the side. Mirror: click the item, then two points of the mirror line (snaps and ortho apply); the source stays. `board.undo` (`how` key|ctrl+z; the last point, else the last change — the sketch's own undo; Ctrl+Z never undoes the job while sketching) / `board.aid` (`key` osnap F3 | ortho F8 | polar F10 45°; ortho and polar exclude each other — the **aids bar** at the bottom of the view, with the typed entry, the status line and Reset view) / `board.snap` (`kind`, `on`, `snaps` the whole list — the arrow beside Snap F3 opens the **snap point list**: endpoint, midpoint, center, geocenter (area centre of a closed shape), quadrant, intersection, extension (a straight line continued past its end, within 0.6 × the aperture), perpendicular, tangent (from the last point to a circle / arc), nearest, parallel (line tool, within 3° of a slanted line; not with Ortho), corner (face / space / cabinet / partition corners, logged as `feature`); kept in settings.json `sketch.snaps`; default off: geocenter, nearest, parallel. F3 still turns all of them off. A "does not snap to X" report is that kind unticked in `sketch.snaps`, or a point snap winning inside the aperture — point snaps beat extension → nearest → parallel → ortho / polar → grid) / `board.dyn.open` (`tool`, `mode`) / `board.dyn.mode` (`mode` polar | delta, `how: "wheel"`) / `board.typein` (`tool`, `mode` polar = length + screen angle | delta = screen dx + dy, `locks` the typed fields, `ok`). Tab or a digit opens two fields beside the cursor once there is a last point: a line takes L + ∠ or dx + dy — the wheel switches while they are open (it does not zoom then); a rectangle takes dx + dy. A typed field holds (orange); the untyped one follows the cursor. Tab moves between the fields, Enter or a click puts the point in, Esc closes them. Sketch-bar tools are icons; the name shows on hover / `board.sketch.finish` (`shapes`, `boards`, `holes`, `open` lines left out — only closed items make boards; overlapping shapes are refused: `Two shapes overlap: trim them into one outline first`). Arcs reach the board as bulges on `params.outline` / `params.holes` (`b`); the generator tessellates them (chords ≤ 0.05 mm off the curve) and marks the board `tessellated`, which the export reports as `quality: tessellated` / `board.back` (`step` sketch | pick, `cleared`) / `board.finish` (one per board: `id`, `index`, `boards`, `points`, `holes`, `stockId`, `colorFace` pull outer | sketch on the sketch plane, `plane`, `pull`, `du`, `dv`, `thickness`, `pose`, `face`) / `board.cancel` (`step`, `how`, `shapes`) / `board.blocked`. A sketch holds several closed shapes: a shape inside another is a **through opening** of it (a `cutout` with a `loop` on face A), a shape inside an opening is a board again, two shapes side by side may share an edge. `blocked` reasons: `no space`, `no room`, `no tool`, `no shape`, `crosses itself`, `the outline crosses itself`, `needs at least 3 points`, `Two shapes overlap: trim them into one outline first`, `An opening touches the shape around it: make it a notch in the outline` (both at Finish sketch), a Fillet / Chamfer / Offset / Extend refusal (its message, e.g. `This corner takes a radius up to 40`), `shorter than 50`, `thicker than the room`. Esc: clears the shape being drawn → drops the tool → (empty sketch only) back to face picking; at the stock step, back to the sketch. Enter / a right-click without a drag ends a line open, takes the cursor as the next point (rectangle corner, circle radius, arc point, mirror line end), or creates at the stock step. A line closes on its first point or with C. Create makes one `sketchBoard` cabinet per outer shape in one undo step. Edit sketch (the Board panel button; `board.edit` `{id, points, holes, arcs, plane, pull}` / `board.edit.finish` `{id, how, points, holes, arcs, from, to}` pose) reopens that one cabinet: outline and holes come back as curves, bulges kept, guides and open lines were not stored. Confirm replaces pose and params in one undo step. A second outer shape is `board.blocked` (`This board is one outline — a second shape would be a new board`). Grooves on `BOARD` shift by the origin move. A rotated pose or a board nudge is `board.blocked` (`this board was rotated` / `this board was nudged`) and the board stays. The board is left out of the 3D view only while that sketch is open. Thickness is the stock, not a drag. Picking the face switches to an orthographic view straight at it (floor and ceiling tilted a hair off ±Z); Create, Cancel, back to picking, or a view button restores the perspective. Orbit, pan, or zoom away and Reset view lights up on the aids bar; `board.view.reset` puts the camera back without leaving the sketch. Object snap takes the sketch's own points, the face's corners first, then any corner in that plane (floor corners on the floor, ceiling corners on the ceiling, cabinet and partition corners in the plane). The last stock and colour face are `job.sketchBoard`, the next board's default. Half grooves are not in this command.
- `bedside.arm` / `bedside.height` / `bedside.blocked` / `bedside.finish` / `bedside.cancel` — Bed Side Table placement (needs the Bedroom body; `blocked` = no body). North-south places **two** tables, one against each wall. Two clicks: first drag is the height (`bedside.height` on the click that locks it; `snap: "fixed panel bottom"` when H landed on the underside of the wardrobe fixed panel, boot height + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197), then the depth into the room. `arm` carries `W` (the wardrobe width), `H`, `snapH`, `D` (default 145). `finish` carries `pair: true` and the left table's id. `bedside.shelf` (`from`, `to`, the middle shelf centreline) / `bedside.zone` (`index` 0 lower / 1 upper, `type` `drawer` | `left_door` | `right_door` in that table's local frame; the other table stores the mirrored hand). The orange bar is `handle: "divider"`, `key: "shelfCenter"`. No back panel. Shelf tongues go through the sides (to the outer face) over the middle third of the depth; each side has a through slot per shelf, 1 mm taller than the shelf and 5 mm longer than the tongue at each end (`ROUTER_DIAMETER_MM` 10) — `SLOT_SHELF_MID` is a hole, the top / bottom slots are outline notches. The bed-side skin is `SHOW` (door stock, outside the carcass under the colour panel; carcass + `SHOW` = wardrobe width). Fronts cover the whole table with one `clearance` at every edge and between them. A nook shelf is not part of this module.
- `rail.group.open` — a rail group's flyout (Bedroom → Body / Bed Box / Bed Side Table) opened on hover or click.
- `resize.start` / `resize.face` / `resize.blocked` / `resize.drag` / `resize.end` / `resize.exit` — the Resize command (S, command bar). Click a face of any module's box (`face` `{axis, dir, label}` in **cabinet-local** axes: `x-` left side, `x+` right side, `y-` front / room face, `y+` back, `z+` top, `z-` bottom); an arrow appears on it; one drag = one undo step, the opposite face stays. `blocked` `reason: "face is fixed"` with `pullable` = the module's `resizeFaces` (floor sides never move; overhead: sides, front, bottom; bedroom body / bed box: the room face only; bed side table: room face + top; lounge: none). `end` carries `from` / `to` `{envelope, pose, zones}` and `stopped` (`minimum N`, `a zone at its minimum`, `the space boundary`, a cabinet / wall id, or the generator error that refused the size — the last good size is kept). Zone rules (`modules.js resizeFace`, `resizeStack`): kitchen side face → a new column once the pull reaches 150 (`left_door` on the left face, `right_door` on the right; a new top zone is `left_door`), a push trims the end column and one under 150 merges into its neighbour; kitchen top → the same on each column's top zone (60); overhead sides → the same with `up_flap` bays (150); small cabinet top → only the top zone, one under 60 merges below; tall top → every zone keeps its share (`fitTallCabinetHeight`); bed side table top → the upper zone (the shelf stays until `attach` pushes it down). A "wrong zone after resize" report is the last `resize.end` `from.zones` → `to.zones`.
- `groove.arm` (`kind`, `shape`, `width`, `depth`) / `groove.face` (`id`, `board`, `face` A|B, `size` `{w, h, t}` face-local) / `groove.point` (`uv`, `snap`) / `groove.add` (`groove` `{id, face, kind, u0, u1, v0, v1, depth, group?}`, `shape` line|rect, `width`) / `groove.remove` (panel ×) / `groove.option` / `groove.size` / `groove.blocked` (`reason` edge face | no size | too deep) / `groove.back` / `groove.cancel` / `groove.exit` — the Groove command (G, command bar; `renderer/grooveTool.js`). Any board of any module, big faces only. Line = two ends of the centreline, axis-aligned by the longer drag, `width` across; Rectangle = two corners. T groove = every rectangle drawn while it is on shares one `group` (`T1`…). Snaps: face corners / edge midpoints / face centre, and corners / centreline ends of that face's grooves; else whole mm (Shift = free). Stored on `cabinet.overrides.boards[roleId].grooves`, merged in `job.resultFor` (`generators/_lib/userGrooves.ts`: feature id `user-G<n>`, `source: "user"`, `for` `user` / `user:T1`), then the milling check re-runs — a groove on the face opposite a hinge cup is a red `both-faces` check and blocks export. A groove that no longer fits after a resize is skipped with a `validation.warnings` line `Groove G1 on <board> … skipped`. Depth leaves at least 1 mm under the groove.
- `measure.arm` / `measure.pick` (`which` anchor|target, `pick` `{kind point|face, label, x, y, z, cabId?, boardId?, faceId?, normal?}`) / `measure.result` (`pair` point-point|point-face|face-face|angle, `distance`, `delta` [dx, dy, dz] for two points, `angle` degrees between two faces, `a` and `b` the picks, `chain` true when Shift continued from the previous point, `how` click) / `measure.clear` (`how` esc|delete|next|changed, `count`) / `measure.exit` (`how`) / `measure.blocked` (`reason` nothing to measure | too many) — Measure (I, command bar; `renderer/measureTool.js`). Temporary: nothing is written to the job and nothing is undone. A point is a board corner (hidden boards are skipped) or a feature point; a face is a board face or a space / cabinet / partition / plane face. Hover lights the point, edge, or face a click would take. A point inside the snap aperture wins, then an edge within about 10 px, then the face under the cursor. One edge reports its length (`pick.length`; an arc is the arc length, not the chord). Two parallel edges, or an edge parallel to a face, give the perpendicular distance; otherwise the angle. Two points give the distance and ΔX ΔY ΔZ; a point and a face, or two parallel faces, give the perpendicular distance (the planes are infinite); two faces that are not parallel give the angle between them, 0 parallel and 90 square. One board face, until the second click, also reads its overall size and thickness. The reading stays until the next one, Esc, Delete, or a job change. Shift on the next click continues from the last point, up to 12. Esc clears, and Esc again leaves the command. Top-level `kind` is the event name, so the measurement is `pair` and the pick's own kind stays inside `pick` / `a` / `b`.
- `board.visibility` (`id`, `boards` role ids, `visible`, `hidden` the cabinet's remaining hidden list) — show or hide boards in the 3D view. Stored on `cabinet.hidden` and saved with the job; a hidden board stays hidden until it is shown again. `V` is the visibility key for whatever is selected: the selected board (a face selection toggles its board), every board of the module when the module itself is selected, or the selected partition (`wall.visibility`). The browser eye toggles one board and does not change the selection. Right-click a module row: Show all boards / Hide all boards.
- `wall.visibility` (`id`, `visible`) — the partition's eye, same `V`. Stored as `wall.hidden: true` and saved with the job. The 3D view skips the wall, its leaf, its pelmet and the yellow cut bar. The floor plan still draws it (faded), and snaps and overlap checks still see it. The browser eye and a right-click Hide / Show do not change the selection.
- `cabinet.add` / `cabinet.remove` / `cabinet.params` / `cabinet.pose` / `cabinet.color` / `select` / `key.delete` / `key.rotate`. `key.delete` from the right-click menu carries `how: "menu"` and `where: "browser" | "3d"` (the job tree or the 3D model). The keyboard omits them. When several cabinets are selected and the clicked one is in that set, `ids` lists them and they go in one undo step. `cabinet.color` (`from` / `to` slot, `name`) is a right-click on a placed cabinet (the browser row or the 3D view) switching it to the job's other door colour; one undo step. A one-colour job has no such item. Boards that are colour B by rule (east-west bedside, bunk lower end and boot flap) stay on `doorColorNameB`. `select` carries `id` (cabinet / wall / plane or null; the primary, last in the set) plus `ids` (every selected cabinet, primary last — one entry after a plain click) plus `board` (role id such as `BP`) and `face` (`A` / `B` / `E<i>`) when the Job tree, a second 3D click or the drawer narrowed the selection to a board / face inside that cabinet — the cabinet is still the selected object; a "clicked the wrong board / face" report is the last `select` with a non-null `board`. `how: "ctrl"` toggles that cabinet in `ids` (3D, the job tree, the floor plan, the board table). A plain click keeps only the one under the cursor. `key.delete` with `ids` longer than one removes that set in one undo step; the panel, handles, Move, Face and `R` stay on the primary.
- **Partition walls / floor plan** (`renderer/floorplan.js`, `walls.js`): `floorplan.open` (`from` button|panel, counts) / `floorplan.close` (`how` button|esc|no space) / `floorplan.tool` (`tool` wall|door|null, `type` showerDoor|slidingDoor for a door) / `floorplan.select` (`id`, `kind` wall|cabinet|null) / `floorplan.blocked` / `floorplan.cancel` (a right-click with nothing to cancel). Drawing (one tool, three steps pt1 → pt2 → offset): `wall.draw.point` (`step` pt1|pt2, `edge` id such as `space:2` or `wall-1:y-#1`, `line` what the user sees — `Left wall` or the partition id, `u`, `length`, `snap` feature|align|grid|locked|`far end`, `alignTo` the partition / cabinet whose centre line / side the point lines up with, `candidates` the lines through the first point, `junction` `{wall, segments}` when the first point sits on a partition standing on the line — both segments beside it stay candidates and the pull direction picks one, `how` click|enter), `wall.draw.typein` (`step`, `value`, `locked`; `how: "tab"` when Tab opened the field), `wall.draw.blocked` (`issues` — e.g. `overlaps cab-1`, `free-standing: neither end rests on a wall`, `outside the space`, or `reason` `shorter than 50`; the wall that was refused), `wall.draw.cancel` (`step`, `reason` esc|right-click|tool off|close), `wall.draw.finish` (`id`, `wall` `{axis, at, u0, u1, side}`, `how` click|enter, `from` the source — a space wall label or the partition id, `edge` the face actually offset from (`wall-1:y-` = its nose face), `offset`, `alignedOffset` partition id when the offset snapped to its centre line, `clamped`, `length` (the board), `drawn` `{u0, u1}` (the picked span, centre line to centre line), `trimmed` `{lo, hi}` — `{id, from, to, by}` per end that was drawn into a partition and pulled back onto its face (a "wall is 9 short" report is this: the point was on wall-1's centre line, the board stops on its face), `anchors` `{lo, hi}` — an end resting ON another partition's face (never inside: no physical overlap, ever). Job level: `wall.add` (the record + the same meta) / `wall.remove` (`id`, `wall`); `key.delete` carries `where: "floorplan"` when pressed on the sheet (with `wallId` when it removed a door, not the wall). **Shower doors** (`floorplan.tool` `door`): `opening.draw.end` (`wallId`, `from` lo|hi, `endU`), `opening.draw.start` (`u`, `offset` from that end, `snap`, `alignTo`), `opening.draw.width` (`u`, `width`, `snap`), `opening.draw.typein`, `opening.draw.clearance` (card opened with the remembered `bottom` / `top`), `opening.draw.blocked` (`step`, `issues` e.g. `new door: overlaps op-1`, `reason` `narrower than 50`, or `no walls`), `opening.draw.cancel` (`step`, `reason` esc|right-click|card cancel|tool off|close), `opening.draw.finish` (`wallId`, `id`, `from`, `offset`, `width`, `bottom`, `top`, `how` enter|ok). Job level: `opening.add` / `opening.set` (`patch`, resulting `opening`) / `opening.remove`. A "door in the wrong place" report: `opening.draw.start` says which end and how far, `opening.draw.width` the width; the record is on the wall (`wall.remove` carries the whole wall incl. `openings`). **Sliding doors** (`floorplan.tool` `door` with `type: "slidingDoor"`; every `opening.draw.*` entry carries `type`): same `end` / `start` / `width` steps, then `opening.draw.side` (`side` ±1 along the wall's axis, `label` front|back|left side…, `pelmet` `{length, stoppedBy: {lo, hi}}` — what the pelmet ran into on that side: a space wall label or a partition id), `opening.draw.clearance` carries `top`, `overlap`, `doorHeight`, `opening.draw.blocked` at `step: "side"` carries the refused `side` and `issues` (`no room to slide open (needs W beside the door leaf)` — W is the hole width minus 100: the open leaf may stick out 100 past the pelmet; only more than that is refused — W is the hole width minus 100: the open leaf may stick out 100 past the pelmet; only more than that is refused, `door leaf H high runs into the ceiling (max …)`, `leaf hits cab-1`, `pelmet hits cab-2`, `door leaf runs past wall-2`), `opening.draw.finish` adds `side`, `sideLabel`, `overlap`, `doorHeight`, `leaf` `{length, z0, z1}`, `pelmet` `{length, height, underside, stoppedBy}`, `cover` (leaf top − pelmet underside; positive = the pelmet hides the track) and `warnings` (`pelmet stops N above the leaf top — the track shows`, a soft warning, never a refusal). The hole's `bottom` is always 0. Leaf / pelmet positions are not stored — they follow `stock.partition.thickness` (leaf 20 clear of the wall face, pelmet 20 clear of the leaf) and the space (pelmet top = roof − ceiling clearance), so a "pelmet too short" report is about the partition it stopped at (`stoppedBy`), and a "track shows" report is `cover` < 0 (leaf top = 15 + `doorHeight`; pelmet underside = wall top − `top`). A "wrong wall" report is normally the last `wall.draw.finish`: `at` is the reference face (`offset` from `from`), `side` the way the thickness grows; the two `wall.draw.point` entries before it say which edge and which snap fixed each end; thickness / height are not in the record — they follow `stock.partition` (`thickness`, `floorClearance`, `ceilingClearance`) in `space.define` / `materials.set`. **Cut into boards** (`walls.js` `wallBoards`): a wall that fits 1200 × 2400 (1220 × 2440 sheet, border removed; either way round) stays one board. Otherwise one butt cut. A sliding door is cut vertically inside the hole, at the midpoint of the part of the hole where both pieces fit. A shower door keeps its hole on one board; the cut is the legal one farthest from the hole, or — when every legal cut goes through the hole — horizontal at the top of the hole, and the checks carry a yellow `joint on the shower door board`. A piece still past 1200 × 2400 is a red check (`P1 is N × N mm`), the wall stays. The yellow bar on the selected wall drags that cut: `wall.split.start` (`id`, `axis` `u`|`z`, `at`) / `wall.split` (`from`, `to`, `changed`, `boards` `[{id, length, height, fits}]`). The dragged value is stored as `wall.split` and the boards are rebuilt; Shift = 1 mm. **Fit to cabinets** (right-click the wall, not a drag; the same menu's Delete removes the partition, `key.delete` `how: "menu"` `where: "3d"`, and the job-tree row's Delete is `where: "browser"`): `wall.fit.start` (`id`) / `wall.fit.pick` (`cabinetId`, `moduleId`, `accepted`) / `wall.fit` (`overheadId`, `kitchenId`, `how` ok|enter) / `wall.fit.cancel` (`how`) / `wall.fit.clear`. The wall stores `fit: { overheadId, kitchenId }` only. The outline then steps along cabinet depth: overhead outer depth + 20, and its lower edge is 15 mm below the overhead door (the door hangs 30 mm below the carcass). A 100 mm neck, and the base total depth + 30 up to 50 above the base. The four step corners are real arcs (a bulge on the outline point), default radius 50. A missing cabinet or a wall that does not run along that depth is a yellow `fitWarnings` line and the wall stays rectangular.
- **Change to waterfall / Change to partition** (`renderer/waterfall.js` plans, `job.js changeToWaterfall` / `changeToPartition`; right-click the partition in 3D or the job tree, or the kitchen / overhead that already carries the pieces). A fitted partition becomes the kitchen's **waterfall** (bench stock, `KITCHEN_RULES.BENCH_THICKNESS_MM` 25, floor → bench top, `params.waterfall` left|right) and the overhead's **end panel** (door stock, `params.endPanel` left|right, board `END_PANEL`: ceiling / carcass top → the door underside (z −30), front flush with the door face, back on the wall). The partition's outer face stays the outer face of both; the partition is removed. Thickness differences come **from the stock**, never a fixed 7 / 2: the kitchen column gives `gap + partition − 25` (`kitchen.delta`, negative = gives), the overhead zone gains `gap + partition − door` (`overhead.delta`); `gap` is how far the cabinet end stood off the partition face (≤ `ABUT_TOLERANCE_MM` 5). With more than one column / zone a quick card asks which one (`renderer/quickCard.js showChoice`; one that cannot give and stay 150 is greyed). A left-end kitchen pose moves to `outer`, a left-end overhead to `outer + door thickness`; right ends keep their pose and grow W. `wall.waterfall` (`id` the wall, `how` menu, `wall` the removed record, `axis`, `outer`, `kitchen` `{id, side, column, delta, pose, length}`, `overhead` `{id, side, zone, delta, pose, cabinetWidth}`, `controlPanels` carried ids) / `wall.waterfall.blocked` (`reason` — also the greyed menu item's tooltip: no fit, a door on the wall, fit warnings, an ensuite, no bench colour, already a waterfall / end panel, the cabinets not square to the wall or on different sides, a gap over 5, or no column / zone able to give). Reverse: `waterfall.partition` (`id` the kitchen or overhead right-clicked, same fields, `wall` the **new** record: `at` = outer, `side` = toward the cabinets, `u0` the kitchen back → `u1` the kitchen front + 30, `fit` set) / `waterfall.partition.blocked` — only when a kitchen waterfall **and** an overhead end panel exist on one axis, same side, outer faces within 1 mm (`partitionPlan same()`); no overhead → no reverse. The kitchen column gains `25 − partition`, the overhead zone gives `door − partition`. Hand wheel arches on a left-end kitchen shift with the carcass. One undo step each way. The overhead envelope is `cabinetWidth + end panel thickness` (`modules.js overheadEndPanelThickness`; `localBox` x0 = −door thickness for a left end panel — the carcass frame never moves), so a "W reads 16 more" report is the end panel. `END_PANEL` is in the door grain group; its through work is listed on the milling face away from the colour (by design, not a `both-faces` issue).
- **Control panel** (an electronic screen recessed through a partition or the overhead's end panel; `generators/_lib/controlPanel.ts`, `generators/overheadCabinet/controlPanel.ts`, `walls.js controlPanelHoles`). Right-click a partition → **Add Control Panel…**, or the overhead whose end panel it would sit on; the kitchen waterfall is refused (greyed: it is bench stock mitred to the bench top). The quick card (`showControlPanelForm`) takes `fromCeiling` / `fromBack` (centre distances, mm; default 200 / 200), `width` 175 (along the wall), `height` 105, `depth` 35 (`CONTROL_PANEL_DEFAULTS`); the same fields are editable rows in the wall page ("Control panels") and the overhead page ("End panel"). Record `{id cp-n, fromCeiling, fromBack, width, height, depth}` on `wall.controlPanels` or on the overhead's `params.controlPanels` with `host: "endPanel"`. The wall's own panels are **derived** into the abutting overhead as `host: "wall"` entries (`job.js syncControlPanels` → `walls.js wallControlPanelsFor`, with `side`, `wall`, `wallThickness`, `fromCeiling` = H − local z, `fromBack` = D − local y; read-only rows "through wall-1 · left end"), so the generator builds the cuts; they are re-derived on every wall / fit / stock / space change and never stored on the cabinet. **Layer rule** (`controlPanelLayers`, "the 10 mm line"), board by board from the room face: the depth still to go ≤ `CONTROL_PANEL_GROOVE_MM` 10 → this board gets a 10 mm half slot (`groove`, running on to the back edge / back wall for the wiring) and the stack stops; otherwise the board is cut through (`cutout`, `through`) and the next board follows; boards are the partition (`wallThickness`) → the overhead end divider `D0` / `Dn` (CPT) → backing dividers `D_CP_L1…` / `D_CP_R1…` (carcass stock, added as full dividers "stuck" behind, each with the usual BP groove / T3 step / screw holes, `for: "D_CP_L1"`) until the depth is reached (18 + 15 + 15 = 48 → a depth 55 puts the slot on the fourth board). On the end panel host the stack starts with `END_PANEL` (door thickness). Beside a **range hood** group the backing divider is the short type standing on `RGHD_TOP` (`D_CP_L1.z0 === RGHD_TOP.z1`) and `RGHD_TOP` gets `RGHD_TOP_<divider>_GROOVE`; no BP groove then. The wall hole is in `wallSolid` → `controlHoles` (`centreU = backU + sign · fromBack`, `centreZ = roof − fromCeiling`); a split never crosses it (`wholeHoles`). `cpanel.add` / `cpanel.set` (`patch`, `from`, `to`) / `cpanel.remove` (`host` wall|endPanel, `id` the wall or cabinet, `panel`, `changed` the overheads re-derived). A "no cut in the overhead" report: the overhead is not against that wall's end plane or the centre is off its carcass (the derived entry is skipped) — check `syncControlPanels` output and the wall checks: `control panel cp-1 is outside the board` is a yellow `fitWarnings` line; `validation.warnings` `runs past the overhead carcass` is the same on the cabinet. Conversions carry panels between the wall and the end panel (`controlPanels` on `wall.waterfall` / `waterfall.partition`). Kitchen boards are never cut by a control panel.
- **Wheel arch on the floor plan** (A; `job.space.params.wheelArches`, drawn red in the plan and as a red volume in 3D — not an `obstacle`, cabinets may stand in it). `floorplan.tool` `tool: "arch"` / `wheelarch.draw` (`step` `rear` | `length` | `width` | `height`) / `wheelarch.draw.blocked` / `wheelarch.draw.cancel` / `wheelarch.draw.typein` / `wheelarch.add` (`id`, `yRear`, `length`, `width`, `height`, `how`, `changed` cabinet ids) / `wheelarch.remove` (`id`, `changed`). One gesture is one symmetric pair. Either rear floor corner: drag along Y toward the nose, the first click is the rear edge (`yRear`, the larger Y), the second is the length toward the nose, the third is the width in from that side wall (the other side mirrors, `wa-n-L` / `wa-n-R`), then a height from the floor. A Kitchen / Ensuite with **Wheel arch avoidance** on, whose carcass back reaches a box, gets `wheelAvoidances` rewritten from it. The checkbox is off until chosen, so a cabinet in the red pair is not cut by itself. A lounge stores the overlap as `planWheelArches`. I and L are notched only where a board sits in a box and get no cover; an end panel outside a box stays a full rectangle, and one inside it stays full height with the corner bitten out. A parallel middle cabinet, when the gap meets a box at the wall, gets `mid_avoidance_top` / `_front` in that overlap and its bottom sits on the top cover. A partition that crosses a box is notched up to that height. Delete on the plan removes the pair.
- `handle.start` / `handle.end` — W/D/H face or `divider` drags with envelope before/after, zones and pose. Handles are hidden while a board / face inside the cabinet is selected. `handle.arm` (`id`, `handle`, `on`, `how: "esc"` when Esc removed it) — an **on-demand** handle (`mod.handlesOnDemand`, today the Bed Box length `D`): the panel's ⇕ button next to the field shows a double arrow just outside that face in 3D; dragging it logs the usual `handle.start` / `handle.end`; it goes on Esc, on a selection change or when pressed again.
- `space.dialog.open` / `space.dialog.close` / `space.dialog.kind` / `space.dialog.choice` / `space.define` — `values` carries the whole form (vehicle: `width`, `rearDepth`, `height`, `frontMode`, `front.points` / `front.dxf.points` as `[y, z]` mm). `space.define` / dialog open+close also carry job-level `finish` (`carcass` White Stipple; `door.series` acrylic|hpl; `door.mode` one|two; `door.colors` `[{id, series, name}]`; `benchTop` `{name}` an HPL decor) and `stock` (`carcass` / `partition` / `door` thicknesses). `space.dialog.choice` on the catalogue uses `key` `finish.door.series`, `finish.door.mode`, `finish.door.sides`, or `finish.benchTop`.
- `materials.set` — the job catalogue was written (`finish`, `stock`), from the Space page on the right. Existing cabinets keep the colour and thicknesses they already copied; new ones inherit. Partition walls read the stock now.
- **Door colour in 3D**: the generators write the cabinet's `doorColorName` (default `Gloss White`) into `finish.colour` on each colour face — door / flap fronts' B face (−Y), tall `T1` / `B1`, overhead `T1`, kitchen `B1`, bedroom colour panels and fronts, small-cabinet fronts, bed-side `SHOW`. The 3D view paints the whole of that board with one flat material from `renderer/doorSwatches.js` (StyleLite screen values; matte / gloss / metallic flakes). No environment reflection in this view. Carcass boards are White Stipple (`renderer/carcassFinish.js`): warm white `F3F3F0` with an even medium stipple, not the old beige. `colour.unknown` (`name`) = a door-stock colour face whose name has no swatch: it shows grey. **Door sides**: job `finish.door.sides` single (default) | double (space dialog "Door sides", `space.dialog.choice` `key: "finish.door.sides"`), copied into new cabinets as `params.doorSides`. Every door-stock board's back big face gets `finish.colour` = the carcass colour (single) or the door colour and grain (double), `stock.sides` 1 | 2 (`generators/_lib/finish.ts applyDoorSides`); carcass stock is always White Stipple both sides. In 3D a single-sided board's back is drawn White Stipple, its front and edges in the door colour. A "door back is white / coloured" report is `doorSides` on the cabinet. Textured HPL decors are 1 m × 1 m images (`renderer/textures/hpl/<name>.jpg`, grain along the image width; Felt Grey has none) laid out in millimetres on the whole board. **Wood grain**: `cabinet.params.grain` `{front, side}` = "horizontal" | "vertical" (missing = module default: kitchen fronts + kick, tall fronts + T1 / B1, overhead flaps + T1, small fronts + door-panel sides, bedroom doors = horizontal; tall colour side panels and bedroom colour panels `WARD_L/R_PANEL` = vertical). The generator writes `finish.grain` (u | v, board-local) on each colour face and, for `doorSeries: "hpl"` only, lists boards past the 1200 × 2400 sheet in `result.grain.issues` (1180 across the grain, 2380 along it; `generators/_lib/grain.ts`) — not validation errors. Those boards are red in 3D and listed in the checks, except a bench top past the sheet (`BENCH`): it stays its colour in 3D and the red line stays in the right-panel checks. `grain.set` (`id`, `group`, `from`, `to`) / `grain.blocked` (same + `boards` `[{id, side, length, limit}]`) = the panel's Wood grain toggle; a blocked switch changes nothing. A handle / Resize drag stops at the last size that added no grain issue (`stopped` names it). `tall.side` (`side`, `from`, `to` none | carcass | colour, `thickness`) = the tall side-panel choice (`left/rightSidePanelThickness` + `left/rightSidePanelFinish`; colour = door stock and thickness, door colour on the outside face). A "wrong colour" report is the cabinet's `doorColorName` (colour A, the upper group) or `doorColorNameB` (colour B, the lower group; a one-colour job copies A into B) in `cabinet.params`, or the swatch table, never the geometry. New cabinets copy their slot: overhead, north-south bedroom (T1 included, door stock), east-west body and the bunk upper end are A; kitchen, ensuite, small cabinet, bedside, lounge, tall, fridge, the bunk lower end and boot flap are B. East-west bedside boards (`BS_SHOW`, `BS_FRONT_HI`, `BS_FRONT_LO`) use B; the other east door boards use A. Lounge seat fronts stay carcass. Existing cabinets keep the colour copied at creation. A "carcass looks beige / smooth" report is the stipple material not applied.
- `materials.default.set` / `materials.default.reset` — "Set current as default" for colours + board stocks: `ok`, `path` (settings.json), `error`, `finish`, `stock`.
- `space.dxf.import` — `ok`, `path`, `points`, `chains`, `units`, `scale`, `counts` per entity type, `warnings`, `range` `{y, z}`; or `error`.
- `space.default.set` / `space.default.reset` — "Set current as default" / "Reset to built-in" on the Define the space dialog footer (Box or Vehicle; the whole form for that kind). `ok`, `path` (settings.json), `error`, `values`. A saved default is what a new space of that kind starts from. Do not rewrite `settings.json` from code — the user saves it in the app.
- `settings.loaded` / `settings.corrupt` / `settings.read.failed` / `settings.write.failed` — user settings file (`<userData>/settings.json`); a corrupt file falls back to built-in defaults and is left in place.
- Placement / move `clamped` may name `roof` (the sloped nose of a vehicle) instead of `ceiling`.
- `file.new` / `file.open` / `file.open.failed` / `file.save` / `file.refresh` (`path`, `cabinets`, `dirty`, `id` — Refresh or F5 reloads the window and keeps the open job; undo starts over) / `file.refresh.failed`, `undo` / `redo`, `view`.
- `file.export` (`path`, `boards`, `materials`) / `file.export.blocked` (`reasons`) — `.cnjob` for OmniCAM. Blocked when any check is red (generator error, grain over the sheet, milling on both faces or the colour face, cabinet outside the space or overlapping a partition or sliding door, illegal wall). Yellow warnings do not block. Hidden boards are included. Edge bands are empty until a module's edges are confirmed. `file.export.step` (`path`, `cabinets`, `boards`, `skipped`) — STEP for Fusion (top bar Export 3D, or right-click Export 3D as STEP…). One file, the ticked cabinets, or the current selection when the cabinet under the cursor is in it. Solids include grooves, LED, hinge cups and through cuts. Red checks do not block it. A board that did not become a closed solid is listed in `skipped` and left out.
- **Generator bench** (`docs/bench-spec.md`; separate window, developer tool): `bench.open` (`module`, `preset`, `from` rail|cabinet|env|tab, `custom`), `bench.tab`, `bench.preset`, `bench.preset.save`, `bench.param` (`key`, `from`, `to`), `bench.select` (`what` board|face|point|joint|key, `id`, `key` / `keys`, `value`, `formula`), `bench.tryout` (`key`, `formula`, `expr`, `from`, `to` — a what-if on one value; nothing was changed), `bench.rule.set` (`name`, `from`, `to`, **`reason`**, `affected` keys, `boards`, `path` — rules.json **was** rewritten), `bench.rebuild` (`ok`, `ms`; the open Cab Lab window then logs `generator.reload` `dir`, `moduleIds`, `cabinets` and regenerates those cabinets without a restart), `bench.pin` (`preset`, `what` board|all, `id`, `count`, `faces`), `bench.report` (`path` of `logs/bench/<time>-<module>.md`, `selection`, `note`), `bench.board.open` / `bench.board.frame`, `bench.view`, `bench.explode` (`mode` assembly|radial, `factor` slider 0..1, `step` null = all boards follow the slider | n = n boards in, `of`, `board` = the one that just went in, `order` = the inferred assembly order, `how` slider|step|key|chip|panel|mode — display only: groups move, boards never change; a "wrong direction / wrong order" report is about `renderer/bench/explode.js` reading `joints` + face features `for`, not about the generator).
  - **Generator Rules editor** (placement rules, `generators/<module>/layout.json`, today OHC T1–T4, and a kitchen / ensuite board once a draft names it — they share `generators/kitchen/layout.json`): `bench.param` (`key`, `from`, `to` — an overall input; never a rule change) / `bench.layout.edit` (`label`, `changes` `[{board, what axis|corner|feature, key, from, to}]`, `moved` boards, `mode` default|face|board, `face` / `corner` / `feature`, `how` type|drag, `when` the switches this edit applies to — absent means every situation) / `bench.layout.refused` (`label`, `errors` — the generator refused the candidate; the draft did not change) / `bench.layout.undo` / `bench.layout.redo` / `bench.layout.discard` (`changes`) / `bench.layout.commit` (`changes`, **`reason`**, `path` — layout.json **was** rewritten; the reason is the user's design intent, keep it) / `bench.mode` (`mode` default|face|null, `board`) / `bench.face.pick` (`board`, `face`, `notch`, `pocket` notch|groove|cutout, `expr`, `step`, `error` — a notch edge, a half-slot wall or floor, or a through-opening wall) / `bench.face.relation` (`board`, `face`, `ref`, `relation` contact|flush) / `bench.face.move` (`on` — M slides a board aside on screen only, not a rule). A "board moved wrong / rule did not stick" report is the last `bench.layout.edit` or `refused`, then whether a `commit` followed (an uncommitted draft lives only in the bench tab).
 - When asked to fix a generator dimension: read the newest `logs/bench/*.md` first — it names the board / point, its provenance chain (`value = formula (terms)`), what was tried and what the user expects. Then `bench.rule.set` entries: the `reason` is the user's design intent; keep it. Pinned values (`generators/<module>/presets.json`) are the protected numbers; run the generator test after any change.
- `generator.errors` — generator `validation.errors` for a cabinet.
- **Milling face / CNC one side**: every board has `milling` A | B | either (`generators/_lib/milling.ts`); partial-depth work must all be on a fixed face, through work is listed on it. `either` = double-sided with no partial-depth work: free to flip when nesting. `result.milling.issues` (`board`, `reason` both-faces | colour-face) = red board + check line, not a validation error. Kitchen V panels (`resolveSlots`): one slot per functional board (several boards on one side of a V are all resolved); half when the V's other face at that height looks into a door / down-flap / open / custom zone (a drawer zone is not visible → through); half slots on both faces → the side with the smaller zone front area (centreline width × zone height, summed) gets none; opposite-face slots < `SLOT_MIN_GAP` 20 apart → the smaller zone's board gets none; a `vPanelMachiningPreferences` entry overrides both. A none board stops flush on the V and gets `result.screws` (3 mm through holes on the V: `SCREW_END_OFFSET` 100 from each end of its depth, the span split at ≤ `SCREW_MAX_SPACING` 150, symmetric; depth < 200 → one in the middle; z = board mid-thickness). A "board has no tongue / screw holes on the V" report is this rule, not a bug.
- `error` / `console.error` — with stack.

## Rules

- Quote the relevant entries (seq + kind + the numbers) when explaining a cause.
- If the files are missing or the last entry is older than the user's report, say so; do not invent events.
- Log payloads are plain JSON; never contain Three.js objects.
